<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<phpunit xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="vendor/phpunit/phpunit/phpunit.xsd"
         bootstrap="vendor/autoload.php"
         colors="true"
         failOnWarning="true"
         failOnRisky="true">
    <!-- The same JIT the CLI turns on in bin/console. The suite spends most of its time in the solver, and the
         tests do not go through bin/console, so it has to be said twice.

         **ON ITS OWN THIS ENTRY DOES NOTHING, AND ON 2026-09-11 IT WAS DOING NOTHING.** `opcache.jit` is
         settable at runtime but `opcache.jit_buffer_size` is `PHP_INI_SYSTEM` and defaults to 0, so with no
         buffer this selects a mode for a compiler with nowhere to emit code. Measured in the project
         container: buffer 0, `opcache_get_status()['jit']['enabled'] === false`. Whether a buffer was there
         in an earlier image is not knowable from here.

         **AND THIS ENTRY IS WHY 0.113.0 CONCLUDED THE JIT WAS WORTH NOTHING ON THIS SUITE.** It measured
         22:06.248 against 22:06.927 and read that as the same number twice. The line below is an `ini_set` that
         runs before any test does, so a run started with `php -d opcache.jit=off` has the JIT switched **back
         on** by this file and both halves of that comparison were traced. Measured three ways on the same 107
         cases: 4.93 s as configured, 5.36 s with `-d opcache.jit=off`, and 6.92 s with
         `-d opcache.jit_buffer_size=0` — the one form `ini_set` cannot undo, because the buffer is
         `PHP_INI_SYSTEM`. Only the third is a genuinely interpreted run.

         **On the real figure the suite gains about as much as the commands do**: the 434 cases of
         `generated/next-event` are 0.2154 s each traced against 0.3304 s interpreted, which is 1.53x. The buffer
         is set at startup in `.ddev/php/opcache-jit.ini` and in `ini-values` on setup-php in
         `.github/workflows/tests.yml`, and that is what actually turned the JIT on.

         **To time an interpreted run, use `-d opcache.jit_buffer_size=0` and nothing else.**

         Kept rather than deleted for the same reason as the call in `bin/console`: it is still what turns the
         JIT on where a buffer exists but no mode is set. -->
    <php>
        <ini name="opcache.jit" value="tracing"/>
    </php>
    <testsuites>
        <testsuite name="unit">
            <directory>tests</directory>
        </testsuite>
    </testsuites>
    <source>
        <include>
            <directory>src</directory>
        </include>
    </source>
</phpunit>
